WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.539
Redis holds three million idempotency keys, all in memory.

2
00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:12.100
When the process restarts, it comes back empty, and every retried payment looks new.

3
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:17.957
The simplest copy is SAVE, which writes every key to a file on the one command thread.

4
00:00:17.957 --> 00:00:23.117
In the lab that took two seconds, and a GET sent meanwhile waited 1.8 seconds.

5
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.572
BGSAVE hands the job to a child.

6
00:00:25.572 --> 00:00:33.596
The main thread calls fork, and the kernel copies only the page table, so both processes point at the same pages.

7
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:38.305
The child writes those pages to a file, while the parent keeps serving clients.

8
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.156
Now a client changes a key on page three.

9
00:00:41.156 --> 00:00:46.599
The kernel first copies that one 4 kilobyte page, and the parent writes to its copy.

10
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:51.561
The child still sees the old page, so its file shows the moment of the fork.

11
00:00:51.561 --> 00:00:56.103
That's copy-on-write, and only pages written during the save get copied.

12
00:00:56.267 --> 00:01:04.252
When it's done, the child renames its file over dump.rdb in one step, so a crash never leaves half a snapshot.

13
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:07.688
On a busy server a snapshot runs about once a minute.

14
00:01:07.688 --> 00:01:12.233
Crash fifty seconds after one, and those fifty seconds of writes are gone.

15
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:18.029
The append-only file logs every write as it happens, and Redis replays it on restart.

16
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:21.461
But a write only reaches the kernel's page cache.

17
00:01:21.461 --> 00:01:26.993
Once a second, a background thread calls fsync, and only then does the disk hold it.

18
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.269
If just the Redis process dies, the kernel still writes the page cache out, and nothing is lost.

19
00:01:33.269 --> 00:01:36.493
If the machine dies, up to the last second is gone.

20
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.132
A snapshot loses every write since the last save.

21
00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:44.265
The log, fsynced each second, loses at most about a second.
